// mgeHost64 — WHAT UNITS DOES THE SCENE TARGET WANT, AND HOW DOES A PASS GET INTO THEM.
// tasks/forge-postprocess.md step 6a.
//
// Until step 6a every colour frag ended `c = tonemap(c)`, so pMSAAColor held DISPLAY-REFERRED values
// whatever its format — which is why the fp16 probe looked identical and why bloom had nothing to
// bloom from. The curve now runs ONCE, at the very end, inside resolve.frag, and the scene target
// carries radiance.
//
// ONE lane decides: gShadowParams.toneParams.x, the host's folded `g_hdrSceneColor && sampleCount>1`
// (shadowparams.h.fsl). A shader must never re-derive it from a format or a sample count — the two
// halves of that condition have to move together, and they stay together because only the host ever
// evaluates them.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE RULE. Every pass that writes to the scene target falls in exactly one of three classes, and
// THIS LIST IS THE CLASSIFICATION — not a plan document, not a commit message. Step 6a shipped once
// with sky.frag in the wrong class, and the whole cost of that was that the rule lived somewhere a
// shader author would never look.
//
//   1. LIT / GENERATED, and it already called tonemap() before 6a   ->  tonemapInPass()
//      opaque, statics, terrain, alpha, multimap, multimap_alpha, water's sun specular.
//      The curve moves out of the pass; the pass writes radiance.
//
//   2. MW's AUTHORED DISPLAY-REFERRED DATA, and it never called tonemap()  ->  liftInPass()
//      sky.frag (MW's own per-vertex atmosphere colours x MW's sky/moon/star/cloud textures),
//      statics_add.frag (MW's glow map), statics_blend.frag (hero blend layers).
//      These reached the target UNCURVED, so moving the curve to the end would apply it to them for
//      the FIRST time and darken them — up to 41/255 at the top end (tonemap(1.0) = 0.84). Lifting
//      them through the inverse means the final curve hands the authored value back untouched.
//
//   3. GENUINELY GENERATED RADIANCE WITH ITS OWN INTENSITY KNOB  ->  neither; write it raw
//      glow.frag (Colour x Intensity x f^2). It is not reproducing anything authored, it WANTS to
//      exceed 1.0 once there is a container for it, and liftInPass would clamp exactly that away.
//      If it reads dim under HDR, the answer is its Intensity knob, not a lift.
//
//   ...plus one thing that is in NO class: statics_multiply.frag writes a MULTIPLIER (DST_COLOR/ZERO
//   dark map), not a colour. A scale factor has no units to convert, so it is left alone — but note
//   that a multiply now lands in scene-referred space, which is a real (and more correct) change.
//
// HOW TO CLASSIFY A NEW PASS, in one question: **does this pass reproduce a value MW authored, or
// does it make one up?** Reproduced -> lift it, and the image cannot move. Made up -> it is radiance;
// let the resolve roll it off.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠ NEITHER FUNCTION DOES ANYTHING IN THE LDR BUILD. Display-referred, tonemapInPass IS tonemap()
// and liftInPass is the identity — i.e. every pass keeps exactly the behaviour it had before 6a, so
// `g_hdrSceneColor = false` stays byte-identical to the pre-6a image. That is the A/B, and it is
// also why a class-2 pass gets liftInPass() rather than "just add the tonemap it was missing": the
// two are the same under HDR but only one of them leaves LDR alone.
//
// Requires (include AFTER opaque.srt.h, AFTER tonemap.h.fsl and AFTER linearize.h.fsl):
// gShadowParams + tonemap()/inverseTonemap() + srgbToLinear()/linearToSrgb()/mod2xLinear(). Both
// maths files deliberately stay free of any SRT dependency, because resolve.frag includes them and
// cannot see gShadowParams — the maths and the policy are separate files precisely so the resolve
// can have one without the other. The includes are listed per shader rather than nested here, which
// is the house convention for .h.fsl and keeps the order visible at each call site.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// STEP 5 — THE SECOND AXIS: WHAT DOMAIN ARE THOSE UNITS IN.
//
// 6a answered "how bright" (display-referred vs scene-referred). It did not answer "in what
// encoding", and the answer was: MW's, i.e. gamma. Step 5 migrates the domain, and the classification
// above is EXACTLY the right place for it, because the question it already asks — *does this pass
// reproduce a value MW authored, or does it make one up?* — is the same question the decode asks.
// Reproduced => it arrived gamma-encoded and must be decoded. Made up => it is already radiance.
//
// ONE MORE LANE DECIDES: gShadowParams.toneParams.y, the host's `g_linearScene && sceneReferred`.
// Same shape, same rule, same reason as toneParams.x — a shader must never re-derive it, and linear
// rides ON TOP of scene-referred because 8-bit linear bands in the darks and a UNORM target cannot
// hold what a linear sum overshoots.
//
// WHERE THE DECODE ACTUALLY HAPPENS, and it is mostly NOT here:
//
//   TEXTURES   — hardware, via _SRGB views (forgerender.cpp's DDS format map). NEVER a shader pow():
//                the hardware decodes BEFORE filtering and a pow() cannot. See linearize.h.fsl.
//   CBUFFERS   — host-side, at the single write site (sun/ambient/fog/zenith/light colours). The
//                fogColNear lift is the established idiom and now composes with it.
//   VERTEX +
//   PER-DRAW   — decodeAuthored() in the VERTEX stage. Decode-then-interpolate is the same ordering
//                rule the hardware follows for texels; decoding in the frag would interpolate in
//                gamma and linearise after, the exact mistake the _SRGB view exists to prevent.
//
// THE ALGEBRA, because it decides what may move. Multiplication commutes with the transfer function
// and addition does not, so every MODULATING stage is preserved and every SUM is where the migration
// lives. MOD is exact; MOD2X needs mod2xLinear() (linearize.h.fsl) and is the one op with a code
// change; ADD moves. The biggest sum in the renderer is `ambient + sun·N·L`, and it moves the most:
// a 0.4/0.6 pair that summed to 1.0 in gamma sums to 0.458 in linear, ~0.70 once re-encoded. Lit
// surfaces where two light terms meet therefore DARKEN, and that is not a regression — it is the
// gamma-space over-brightening that abot's Darkening mod has been cancelling by hand all along
// (its caveLightnessPerc 42 lands inside the same numbers). Dropping abot and going linear is
// close to a wash, which is why the plan makes them two separately-measured steps.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#ifndef SCENE_COLOR_H
#define SCENE_COLOR_H

// Class 1. The curve, still in the pass, until the scene goes scene-referred.
float3 tonemapInPass(float3 c)
{
    return (gShadowParams.toneParams.x > 0.5f) ? c : tonemap(c);
}

// Class 2. The mirror image: nothing to do while the target is display-referred, and a lift into
// scene-referred units when it is not, so resolve.frag's curve returns the authored value.
//
// The [0,1] clamp inside inverseTonemap() is CORRECT here rather than merely safe: the target this
// reproduces was B8G8R8A8_UNORM, which clipped at 1.0, so anything a class-2 pass pushed above 1
// was never on screen. Lifting the CLIPPED value reproduces what was actually displayed.
float3 liftInPass(float3 c)
{
    if (gShadowParams.toneParams.x <= 0.5f) { return c; }
    // Step 5. The lift's CONTRACT is unchanged — "make the end of the frame hand this authored value
    // back untouched" — but the end of the frame now runs tonemap(linearToSrgb(x)), so the value that
    // has to reach it through the encode is inverseTonemap(authored), and the authored value must
    // first be recovered out of the linear domain this pass is already working in (the texel came
    // through an _SRGB view; the vertex colour through decodeAuthored). Hence the round trip: encode,
    // lift, decode. Composition, not a correction — each half is exactly the transform it says.
    if (gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f) {
        return srgbToLinear(inverseTonemap(linearToSrgb(c)));
    }
    return inverseTonemap(c);
}

// ─── Step 5: the domain ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Is the scene target LINEAR? For a pass that needs to branch on it structurally. Note this is a
// cbuffer bit, i.e. wave-uniform but NOT compile-time — DXC will not strip what it guards
// ([[project_uniform_branch_is_not_free]]), so guard whole blocks, never single instructions.
bool sceneIsLinear()
{
    return gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f;
}

// MW authored this colour for a gamma display. Put it in the scene's domain.
//
// VERTEX STAGE ONLY for anything interpolated (vertex colours, and the per-draw material lanes that
// ride the instance stream beside them). Decode-then-interpolate; never the reverse.
float3 decodeAuthored(float3 c)
{
    return (gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f) ? srgbToLinear(c) : c;
}

// Scalar twin — MW's per-vertex emissive STRENGTH (distant statics pack it in Normal.w and add it as
// a grey colour), which is authored on the same display scale as a colour channel and converts the
// same way. ALPHA NEVER COMES HERE: coverage is not a colour and has no transfer function.
float decodeAuthored1(float c)
{
    return (gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f) ? srgbToLinear1(c) : c;
}

// MW's MODULATE2X stage, in whichever domain the scene is in. The literal 2 is a display-space
// number — see mod2xLinear() in linearize.h.fsl for why a constant cannot replace it under the
// piecewise curve. `t` is the SAMPLED texel, i.e. already decoded by the _SRGB view when linear.
float3 mod2xStage(float3 t)
{
    return (gShadowParams.toneParams.y > 0.5f) ? mod2xLinear(t) : (t * 2.0f);
}

#endif // SCENE_COLOR_H
